CVE-2026-64560 — reachability measured on a live device
=======================================================
device      panther  google/panther/panther:17/CP2A.260705.006/15641320:user/release-keys
kernel      6.1.157-android14-11-gbd23337e42e7-ab14791245  (GS201 PANTHER MP)
captured    2026-08-31, uptime 2d10h, KernelSU loaded (u:r:ksu:s0), panic_on_warn=0
_text       0xffffffdbc3a00000        posix_cpu_timer_del  0xffffffdbc3b951f4 (+0x1951f4)
ground truth for the disassembly: boot_b (slot _b, /dev/block/sda20, 64 MiB, boot hdr v4)
            kernel_size 0xfd6670, LZ4-legacy -> Image 35633664 B (see posix_cpu_timer_del.disasm)

VERDICT: VULNERABLE. The exploitable state (armed timer freed while still linked in the
process timerqueue) was reached on this build, and the kernel said so itself.

1. Vulnerable branch located and verified against this build's own code
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  +0x040  bl   cpu_timer_task_rcu
  +0x044  cbz  x0, +0x0ec              ; p == NULL -> out
  +0x054  bl   __lock_task_sighand
  +0x058  cbz  x0, +0x128              ; <== sighand == NULL: the race branch
  +0x128  ldr  x8, [x19, #152]         ; ctmr->head          (k_itimer.it=0x78 + cpu_timer.head=0x20)
  +0x12c  cbz  x8, +0x138
  +0x130  brk  #0x800                  ; WARN_ON_ONCE -> posix-cpu-timers.c:495
  +0x134  b    +0x0ec                  ; out, timer NOT dequeued
  +0x138  ldr  x8, [x21]               ; x21 = &ctmr->node   (RB_EMPTY_NODE check)
  +0x13c  cmp  x8, x21
  +0x140  b.eq +0x0ec                  ; not queued -> clean
  +0x144  b    +0x130
BTF corroboration (data/live/.../btf-vmlinux):
  k_itimer.it = 0x78 (size 0x108/264)   cpu_timer.node = 0x00, .head = 0x20  -> head at +152  OK
  signal_struct.posix_cputimers = 0x120

2. Measured on device (kprobes; exploit/cve64560/trigger, 8-core, exec cpu7, del cpu2-6)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  probe                     what it means                                 count
  pcd_armed  +0x084         delete found timer queued (normal path)      184799 / 15 s
  pcd_null   +0x128         sighand == NULL observed (race window won)   16 / 40 s + 7 / 15 s
  pcd_null2  +0x128 w/args  same, with ctmr->head fetched                 9 / 40 s
  pcd_queued +0x144         head==NULL but node queued                        0   (wrong probe, see below)
  Rounds: 62/s. Race window: ~0.4 wins/s, ~1 in 3e4 deletes.
  All 9 arg-captured hits had head != 0, e.g.
    head=0xffffff881be05258 itclock=0xfffc8eba itid=0x2852   (itclock & 7 == 2 == CPUCLOCK_SCHED,
    upper bits = ~victim_pid -> a process-wide CPU clock on ANOTHER process, as intended)

3. Kernel-side proof
--------------------
  [209036.145760] ------------[ cut here ]------------
  [209036.145800] WARNING: CPU: 4 PID: 17823 at kernel/time/posix-cpu-timers.c:495 posix_cpu_timer_del+0x130/0x150
  [209036.147288] CPU: 4 PID: 17823 Comm: cve64560-trigge ... 6.1.157-android14-11-gbd23337e42e7-ab14791245 #1
  [209036.147339] pc : posix_cpu_timer_del+0x130/0x150     lr : posix_cpu_timer_del+0x58/0x150
  [209036.147516] x21: ffffff8866c78180  x19: ffffff8866c78108      ; x21 == x19+0x78 == &ctmr->node
  [209036.147586] x8 : ffffff88fd5aed58                             ; ctmr->head != NULL == still queued
  [209036.147648] Call trace: posix_cpu_timer_del+0x130/0x150 <- __arm64_sys_timer_delete+0x6c/0x1bc
  posix-cpu-timers.c:495 is WARN_ON_ONCE(ctmr->head || timerqueue_node_queued(&ctmr->node)).
  It fired on the FIRST trigger run. Device did not crash; no pstore entry was produced.

4. Consequences for a port
--------------------------
  * The freed object comes from a DEDICATED cache, not kmalloc:
      /proc/slabinfo: posix_timers_cache  903/1023 objs, objsize 264, 31/slab, 2 pages/slab, 33 slabs
      kernel/time/posix-timers.c:276  SLAB_PANIC | SLAB_ACCOUNT
      release_posix_timer() -> call_rcu(k_itimer_rcu_free) -> kmem_cache_free(posix_timers_cache)
    So reclaim is either same-type (another k_itimer, partial control) or a cross-cache
    attack (drain a slab -> page back to buddy). This is NOT a kmalloc-256/512 spray.
  * The dangling node lives in victim->signal->posix_cputimers.bases[SCHED].tqhead and is
    walked on the victim's group exit (cleanup_timers) and on every expiry check.
  * +0x144 is NOT a useful oracle: head is always non-NULL first, so +0x12c never falls
    through. Use +0x128 with head=+152(%x19), or just the WARN.
